## Conditional probability

### The idea

**Conditional probability** asks: once I know a salesperson is a top
performer, what's the chance they're promoted? We write it **P(promoted
\| performance = 4)** — the "\|" means *given*.

To compute it from data you don't need a formula. Just **restrict to
the group** and take the mean.

```{r conditional-performance}
salespeople |>
  group_by(performance) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    p_promoted_given_performance = mean(promoted)
  )
```

### Knowing something changes everything

::: callout-note
Same company, same promotion process — but a very different, and far
more useful, probability once you condition on performance. Good
prediction is mostly about finding the right things to condition on.
:::

